body:
Ok, I have spend the day on this, dived into some Bullet code, done some 4.x experiments, 3.x experiments, figured out how Godot Physics is actually working and got started on understanding Bullet better (but not finished with that). There is always 'just one more thing to check', so it tends to drag on. 

### Static body removal is not working for me with this PR
I changed my local MRP to try to test the static body removal fix you are showing above. I can't get that to work with my MRP, and I am puzzled as to how this PR could fix the body removal problem. My MRP is calling queue_free() on the static body, and the rigid body sitting on top of it remains there (with this PR on 3.x).

* Could you please share the MRP from the videos you are showing? Or maybe fill me in if I am missing something?

### I would like to understand what Bullet is doing
I have been trying to ascertain what the problem might be in Bullet, and also how Bullet itself wakes objects (before narrow phase, or after narrow phase). Ultimately my concern is that we are waking based on broadphase only. I think that is fine, but perhaps not so awesome if Bullet's normal behaviour is to wake after narrow phase (which is less work). 

move_and_slide() seems to wake after it's own narrow phase, so it is not adding any unnecessary workload. 

I haven't found the answer yet and the Bullet code is not making it easy. Since the answer always seems tantalisingly close, I kind of don't want to just pass the review without just first looking into one more thing.

I have found where I think Bullet is trying to make a KinematicBody do the right thing: in `BtSimulationIslandManager.cpp`, line 316.

This code looks like it is trying to wake objects due to a KinematicBody intersection, but it's clearly not too sure of itself. By the time we get to this code, the KinematicBody is simply not on an island, so this code isn't able to do what it should. Somewhere in the Island initiation and building phase, the KinematicBody is not being added, and I suspect that it is because only an active RigidBody can initiate an island (not a KinematicBody). 

I wanted to test this further, but I am running out of time today, so just sharing it here in case you wanted to look at it during the 'night shift'.

I believe that a KinematicBody should be able to initiate an island, but that is probably lost in an un-covered edge case. KinematicBodys cannot connect islands, so I suspect that the code that guards a KinematicBody against connecting an island is also inadvertently guarding against it initiating one. Since the KinematicBody in our example is always on it's own, and all the RigidBodies around it are asleep, it needs to form an island on it's own in order to even reach the code below. 

If this were the source of the bug, then if the Kinematic Body had another active rigid body attached to it, then it should be able to wake things.

If it is a Bullet bug, then fixing the Bullet bug is a more obvious thing to do, and it's hard for me to look away, considering that the code in Bullet does look like it wants to do the right thing.

```cpp
		///@todo: check sleeping conditions!
		if (((colObj0) && colObj0->getActivationState() != ISLAND_SLEEPING) ||
			((colObj1) && colObj1->getActivationState() != ISLAND_SLEEPING))
		{
			//kinematic objects don't merge islands, but wake up all connected objects
			if (colObj0->isKinematicObject())// && colObj0->getActivationState() != ISLAND_SLEEPING)
			{
				if (colObj0->hasContactResponse())
					colObj1->activate();
			}
			if (colObj1->isKinematicObject()) // && colObj1->getActivationState() != ISLAND_SLEEPING)
			{
				if (colObj1->hasContactResponse())
					colObj0->activate();
			}
			if (m_splitIslands)
			{
				//filtering for response
				if (dispatcher->needsResponse(colObj0, colObj1))
					m_islandmanifold.push_back(manifold);
			}
		}
```

### In the absence of a deeper understanding of the issue within Bullet, I think this PR is ok
I think if it came down to it, and we couldn't reasonably ascertain what Bullet is doing and why, I think this PR is still ok, because while it might be waking before narrow phase, that is what Godot Physics does. 

There is a chance of a minor behaviour change somewhere that might matter in some way, but ultimately no one is force-pushing a version upgrade.

### Still not a fan of making it optional
I still don't favour the 'make it optional' approach over just taking an informed risk. Making it optional adds interface and kind of handballs a deprecation decision to our future selves (who will be less informed than our current selves). If the 'option' defaults to off, then our future selves will have little more testing feedback than we have right now.

The second part of my concern is just the design of the API that makes this optional. If we have something called 'Wake Neighbours' then you would want it to do that when it is on, and not do that when it is off. Neither 'off' nor 'on' would actually behave quite the way the user would expect. There are plenty of features like this in Godot, but I would feel obligated to do an appropriate amount of hand wringing or bike shedding before adding another one ;-).

> Could you please share the MRP from the videos you are showing? Or maybe fill me in if I am missing something?

The reason is that in the MRP, I'm moving the the platform, not deleting it. Moving it calls `body_set_translate()`, which calls awake on the previous pairings. If you delete it, the awaken (so far) never gets called. But it may be a one liner to call the `awake_neighbours` when deleting a body, I'll take a look, perhaps we can fix this at the same time.

UPDATE: Yes was trivial to fix, just needed a call to awake neighbours when removing from a space:

```
void RigidBodyBullet::set_space(SpaceBullet *p_space) {
	// Clear the old space if there is one
	if (space) {
		// Ensure any bodies touching the object to be removed
		// are woken, so that e.g. boxes fall from deleted platforms.
		space->rigid_body_wake_neighbours(this);
```

I've added the MRP to the PR first post above, in case you want to test.

...

Your investigations seem to be along the right track, but I'm also of the view that perfect is the enemy of good enough, and we should go for this in the meantime, and then regard only awaking the narrow phase as a `todo`. Indeed, it might even be possible to do a simple object / object collision check in the `awaken_neighbours`.

There are trade offs here though too, in that actually _doing_ the narrow phase check has to be outweighed by the performance gains from not awakening the odd object that is within the AABB but not actually being hit.
